          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My child has a temperature of 39.5 and a rash that does not go pale when I press a glass on it. How urgent is this, and what should I do right now?",
          "answer": "**This could be an emergency. Call your local emergency number now, or take your child to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it improves.**\n\nThe automated check labelled this \"routine,\" but that label does not fit the guidance it returned. The meningitis guidance (*Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*, educational summary) says to call emergency services immediately for \"a rash of small red or purple spots or bruises that does not fade when pressed,\" tested by rolling a clear glass over it. It lists fever alongside that rash as a warning sign. The sepsis guidance (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*) also lists \"a rash that does not fade\" as a sign in a child. Both documents say these conditions can worsen over hours.\n\nWhile you are getting help, these signs also need immediate attention, according to the meningitis guidance: a high-pitched or moaning cry, irritability when handled, refusing feeds, a bulging soft spot on the head, unusual drowsiness or difficulty waking, floppiness or stiffness, pale, blotchy or mottled skin, fast breathing, or a seizure.\n\nTell the emergency staff about the rash and the temperature, and say clearly, \"Could this be sepsis or meningitis?\" The sepsis guidance notes that asking this question prompts a structured assessment.\n\nI cannot tell you what is causing this. Only a clinician who examines your child can do that. The other documents returned by the search, on heart failure and the common cold, do not apply here.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
